In addition to the EFI system partition, a Linux extended boot loader partition
(XBOOTLDR) can be created. This allows separating the files required for booting
Linux separate from the boot loader. The boot loader will reside on the
EFI system partition, kernel and initramfs files will reside on XBOOTLDR.

If you already have an EFI system partition of adequate size, creating an
additional XBOOTLDR partition is not recommended. Having two separate boot
partitions will then only add complexity without any benefits. The main use case
for creating XBOOTLDR is extending an EFI system partition that is too small
to reliably host kernel and initramfs files. This is often the case, if it was
created by Windows.

The XBOOTLDR partition can have any file system that your UEFI implementation
can read. For compatibility reasons, this script will nevertheless always format
the partition with FAT32.